# Per-module tonal variation v02

Built-in image_gen edit of Polar_Distant_v01. Grayscale shade artwork only, not packed texture or coverage. Layout approximately preserved by generative editing, not guaranteed pixel-identical. Module contrast varies irregularly; outer rim location is an artistic apparent-distance cue, not physical distance. Upper and right groups have compressed interior shading, bottom groups retain richer contrast. Actual sky blending requires separate coverage and shader palette integration; not yet applied to Unreal. Randomness is a fixed painted distribution, not runtime animation.

## Initial prompt

Edit this exact grayscale polar UV cloud texture. Preserve the entire composition, all cloud silhouettes, positions, sizes, orientation, black empty areas and black background. Modify ONLY interior shading/value ranges of existing cloud groups. Maintain the hand-painted gouache brushwork. The center is zenith and outer rim is horizon; radius is a compositional proxy for apparent distance, not actual physical depth. Introduce strong but tasteful atmospheric tonal hierarchy: outermost small distant cloud groups should have substantially compressed INTERNAL contrast, with charcoal shadows lifted toward their mid-gray and brightest highlights subdued. They should appear flatter and quieter in value, NOT simply uniformly darkened and NOT dissolved into black. The few taller inward-reaching cloud masses can retain moderately richer shading but never uniformly deep-black interior shadows in all modules. Crucially give individual cloud modules irregular distinct tonal ranges even at the same radius: alternate softly muted midgray groups, slightly lighter low-contrast groups, and a few moderately contrasted accents, in an organic non-repeating distribution. Variation belongs to each entire coherent cloud module, NOT pixel noise, NOT speckles, NOT a radial band or gradient over the whole image. Aim for 3 broad families of narrow/mid/moderate shading range while retaining subtle painted detail. No new clouds, no deleting clouds, no changes to geometry, no blur of silhouettes, no rings, no text or labels, no color. Strict grayscale square black-backed source artwork. This is a shade-source revision; preserve silhouette coverage independently in appearance.

## Refinement prompt

Edit ONLY the grayscale shading of this polar cloud artwork. Keep black background and EXACT existing cloud placement and silhouettes. Make the variation in value ranges MUCH MORE OBVIOUS at cloud-module level. Currently all modules look similarly high contrast; correct that. Treat the circular layout as a clock: the large cloud at 12 o'clock must be a SOFT LOW-CONTRAST midgray mass with its internal shadows almost lifted to its highlights (approximately gray values 90-120 out of 255). The cloud group at 9 o'clock should have moderate contrast (roughly 60-145). The one at 3 o'clock should be a quiet light-gray low-contrast cloud (110-140). The bottom 6 o'clock group retains the richest shadow range (40-155), without changing its shape. Small distant fragments in the outer ring vary irregularly between very subdued dark-midgray narrow ranges (55-80), midgray narrow ranges (85-115), and a few lighter ones (110-140). Inner wisps remain delicate. Do NOT darken all modules together, do NOT introduce noise or erase silhouettes. Keep painterly stroke structure but greatly compress contrasts within the quiet modules; most clouds should no longer have deep charcoal interiors. Important: black pixels outside clouds remain pure black, not gray fog. This is grayscale shade artwork with deliberately different value ranges for different cloud modules, NOT alpha coverage. No labels or text.
